WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.255
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:01.255 --> 00:00:02.880
FILIP JAN DEPAUW:
Lighting is something

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:04.500
which is pretty static.

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.510
You think about it when
it doesn't work anymore.

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.250
The change with LED
is that it not only

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.390
became greener and
more sustainable,

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:12.510
but it became intelligent.

00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:15.060
GEORGE YIANNI: Philips Hue is
an internet-connected lighting

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:15.790
solution.

00:00:15.790 --> 00:00:18.373
FILIP JAN DEPAUW: You can steer
your lighting with your voice,

00:00:18.373 --> 00:00:19.890
have the lights
change with music

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:22.650
when you have a party, as
well as an element of comfort

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:23.657
and security in it.

00:00:23.657 --> 00:00:25.490
GEORGE YIANNI: When we
launched Philips Hue,

00:00:25.490 --> 00:00:27.820
we didn't know whether
we needed infrastructure

00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:29.780
for 1,000 people or
a million people.

00:00:29.780 --> 00:00:31.590
We wanted it just
to scale and work.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.110
FILIP JAN DEPAUW: So our choice
of partners was fundamental.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.080
We needed that scalability,
that back end, that robustness,

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:38.160
that maturity.

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:39.690
That's why we selected Google.

00:00:39.690 --> 00:00:41.940
GEORGE YIANNI: We've worked
with the Google Cloud team

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:42.990
since the very beginning.

00:00:42.990 --> 00:00:45.720
At the moment, we're running
on the Google Container Engine.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.210
This is working
fantastically for us.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:51.180
By breaking our application
down into containers,

00:00:51.180 --> 00:00:54.480
we have all these small parts
that we can deploy, release,

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.060
test independently, and
that's allowed us to move

00:00:57.060 --> 00:00:59.910
to much faster release cycles.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.970
We were able to spend less time
tinkering with infrastructure

00:01:02.970 --> 00:01:06.060
and more time focusing on the
applications and the use cases

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.020
for consumer.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.180
We are really getting
close to the cutting

00:01:09.180 --> 00:01:10.095
edge of what's there.

00:01:10.095 --> 00:01:11.970
FILIP JAN DEPAUW: We're
growing tremendously,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:13.469
and we want to make
sure that we can

00:01:13.469 --> 00:01:16.380
deliver the lighting experience
we want in every single home.

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:20.330
[MUSIC PLAYING]

